// Naive schedule for example 03-reduction.
//
// Single worker `host`, every kernel placed there. Smoke test for the
// algorithm/schedule split end-to-end against this algorithm file.
// If `03-reduction/prog.algo.nuc` parses, lowers, links, and emits
// reference-matching bytes under this schedule + pthreads-sync, the
// two-phase reduction shape is sound at M1.
//
// No `transfer` directives: with everything on one worker no data
// crosses worker boundaries. PRD §6.3.4 only requires `transfer` for
// cross-worker edges. The link pass enforces that.
//
// No loop transforms: no `block=`, no `vectorize=`, no `partition=`.
// The point of the smoke test is "the algorithm runs sequentially
// and produces the reference"; the distributed schedule (see
// schedules/distributed.sched.nuc) is where the partition-axis
// schedule actually flexes — and is shipped as a stretch because
// per-partition transfers are not yet on the codegen path
// (TASK-0126).

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host };

    place load_input  on host;
    place save_output on host;
    place accumulate  on host;
    place combine     on host;

    // No loop transforms.
    // No transfer directives — no cross-worker edges.
}
